# Verweis Release Notes

Verweis was called Zetteling until 5.2.0; entries below that predate the
rename keep the old name. The full, user-facing history is on the About
page inside the app (src/Verweis/templates/about.html). Engineering notes
are in DEVNOTES.md.

## 5.2.0 (November 2026)

* Renamed Zetteling to Verweis (German for a cross-reference). Package
  src/Verweis, app ID com.shivissar.verweis, homepage
  https://shivissar.com/verweis, repository shivissar/Verweis. On first
  launch, migrate.py moves the old app-data folder
  (com.shivissar.zetteling) into the new one and repoints settings that
  named places inside it; the hidden .zetteling-vault.json and
  .zetteling-codebook.json in the notes folder are renamed to .verweis-*
  (the old names are still read until then). The locked-note encryption
  labels (zetteling-vault-v1, zetteling-locked-v1, the BEGIN/END marker,
  HKDF info strings) keep the old name: changing them would make existing
  locked notes unreadable. New "V" icon in the style of the old "Z".

* Note history (history.py): a copy of the file before it is overwritten,
  at most every 10 minutes while editing and always before restore, lock,
  link-mention, code rename/merge/removal and outline changes; up to 200
  per note in <app data>/history/<id>/. History page with line diff and
  restore (which saves the current version first). Locking a note encrypts
  its plain-text versions.
* Backlinks show the sentence around the link. Unlinked mentions (title or
  its main part before " — " / ": ") with one-click linking that keeps the
  source wording as [[id|text]].
* Templates (note_templates.py): .templates/ in the notes folder, seeded
  with interview memo, fieldnote, reading note, lecture plan; picker on new
  notes, palette commands, Settings → Templates (edit, new, delete,
  restore built-ins); {{date}} {{time}} {{weekday}}.
* Qualitative coding (codebook.py): [passage]{.code .code2} spans
  (pandoc bracketed spans), rendered highlighted with code chips; ⇧⌘C code
  picker; `codes` index table (unlocked notes only; locked notes read live
  while open); Codebook and code pages with definitions, categories,
  co-occurrence, rename/merge, remove, CSV export.
* Manuscripts: notes of type manuscript; ![[id]] embed lines render the
  embedded note (two levels) in any note; outline panel (move, remove, add
  note, add heading, add thread); export expands embeds.
* Search filters: kind:, tag:/-tag:, thread:, code:/-code:, after:,
  before:, is:locked/unlocked, "phrase", -word, in the search page, palette
  and link picker.
* Welcome tour: three cards on the method first (the slip-box idea,
  four habits, how Zetteling supports them; "Go straight to the app"
  skips them), then a short spotlight tour of the window (library, new
  note and templates, search, note list, edit, lock, history, export,
  side panel, writing shortcuts, browse, settings). Starts by itself once
  (settings.tour_seen, saved when it opens), again from the compass button
  in the sidebar, /tour or the palette. Skips parts not on the page;
  arrow keys, Esc, focus kept in the card; respects reduced motion.
* Update check (updates.py): once a day, if on (settings.update_check,
  default on; a tickbox on the welcome screen; Settings → Updates), an
  unauthenticated GET to api.github.com/repos/shivissar/Zetteling/releases/
  latest on a background thread started by the first main page. Remembers
  the answer in settings; retries after 3 h on failure. Sidebar notice and
  /update page (notes as escaped text, Download / release page opened in
  the browser with `open`, only for this repo's GitHub URLs; Skip this
  version; install steps including xattr). Check now in Settings and the
  palette. Nothing is downloaded or installed by the app. Privacy page,
  Help → Updates and INSTALL.md updated.
* Outside links open in the browser: a capture-phase click handler sends
  http(s) and mailto links to other sites (in notes, Privacy, Settings →
  AI, credits) to POST /open-link, which runs `open` after checking the
  scheme (updates.outside_link). Before, Toga's WebView loaded them in
  the app window with no way back. Zetteling's own links are unchanged.
* Back button on Help, Export (and the exported page) and Open beside
  (picker and side-by-side view): returns to the note the page is about
  or the note you came from (else the page you came from); esc does the
  same, after closing any open palette or picker, and in a form field the
  first esc only leaves the field. Exports of a thread or tag go back to it.
* Recovery key: Print (which does nothing in the app window) replaced by
  Save as text file (Downloads, never the notes folder; mode 600; Finder
  shows it) and Copy.
* Credits: Niklas Luhmann and Aneesh Aneesh link to their Wikipedia
  pages, and Shiv Issar to www.shivissar.com (Settings → About, About,
  Help, Licence).
* settings.save is serialised and writes the file atomically (temp file
  + replace), since the update check saves from a background thread.
* Help page rewritten: grouped sections, sticky contents sidebar that
  follows your place, shorter text, new features documented.
* Fixes: exports no longer pass [[id]] links to pandoc as raw brackets;
  single-note exports no longer repeat the title; notes created in the same
  second get distinct IDs.

### Audit fixes (8 November)

* Search results escape note text, keeping only the <mark> highlights
  (a note's HTML used to run from the search page).
* Note HTML is sanitised by default with nh3 (ammonia), replacing bleach;
  footnotes, table alignment, coded passages and wikilinks survive. Turned
  on once for existing installs (settings.html_safety_applied).
* Content-Security-Policy with a per-request nonce on every page; inline
  event handlers moved to app.js (data-confirm, data-history-back);
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff.
* TRUSTED_HOSTS = 127.0.0.1, localhost (ZETTEL_TRUSTED_HOSTS to change);
  ProxyFix only with ZETTEL_BEHIND_PROXY=1; random SECRET_KEY.
* Notes are written atomically (temp file, fsync, replace; permissions
  kept).
* Recovery-key file: falls back from Downloads to the home folder and
  reports a failure instead of a server error.
* Locked notes: the chip reads "Locked · open" (a button that closes them)
  or "Locked · closed"; a sidebar button while open; a notice after
  locking. Plain-text notes of always-locked kinds are locked while the
  vault is open (on unlock, then at most once a minute on page loads),
  except ones whose lock was removed on purpose (settings.lock_exempt).
* Argon2id: RFC 9106's second recommended setting (64 MiB, t=3, p=4) for
  new vaults; vaults made with the 2 GiB setting are re-wrapped on the next
  unlock, and the header copy in each locked note is refreshed.
* Export warning counts locked notes embedded in exported ones.
* Interrogate writes out embedded notes (three levels) and turns links
  into titles; embedded locked notes are replaced by a placeholder.
* Quitting: App.on_exit asks the page (window.__zettelQuit, a synchronous
  save) and shows a dialog if anything couldn't be saved.
* Start-up: the window opens at once with a loading page; the Flask app is
  imported and served from a background thread on a port bound by
  make_server; an error page replaces the 10 s timeout crash. The start-up
  resync skips files whose mtime matches the index (index_version guards
  upgrades).
* Embedding after a save runs on one background worker that re-reads the
  note and drops the vector if it was locked meanwhile.
* Codebook CSV: POST /codes/export writes to the export folder and reveals
  it (the window can't download); cells starting with = + - @ get a
  leading apostrophe.
* net.ssl_context(): certifi for Voyage, Ollama over https and the update
  check.
* tests/browser: the Playwright suites, fixture, run_all.py and README.
* ⇧⌘N (GET /journal/new) no longer creates a file: it opens a new-note
  form titled with the date and time, kind journal, body focused; the
  first save creates the entry. New notes of a locked kind hide tag
  suggestions, as locked notes do.
* Note files whose front matter can't be parsed are listed
  (watcher.PROBLEMS): a sidebar notice and Settings → Folders, with a
  plain-English reason; Help → Troubleshooting.
* settings.json: every save also writes settings.json.bak; an unreadable
  file is read from the backup (else defaults), kept as
  settings.json.unreadable-<stamp> on the next save, and reported in the
  sidebar and Settings.
* Settings → Trash and history: Empty trash (deletes .trash notes and
  their history unless the ID is back in the folder) and Delete their
  history (history of notes neither in the folder nor the trash). Nothing
  is deleted automatically.
* One Markdown converter per request thread (footnotes and other
  per-document state could mix between two pages rendered at once).
* A note changed outside Zetteling since the editor opened it (or since
  Zetteling last wrote it) is kept in its history, reason "external",
  before an in-app save overwrites it, whatever the 10-minute spacing;
  the editor says so.
* Templates: a notes folder where .templates can't be created no longer
  breaks pages; the built-in templates are used, and saving, deleting or
  restoring templates reports the problem.
* Logs: no request log in the Mac app (it recorded search words and note
  IDs in the system log); console messages name notes by ID, never by
  filename, and search errors don't quote the search.
* Help and INSTALL: macOS asks again for folder access after each update
  of an ad-hoc-signed build; iCloud Drive's Optimise Mac Storage (Keep
  Downloaded on macOS 15+). pyproject icon path uses the package's case.
* Licence page: the plain-English summary now matches PolyForm
  Noncommercial's permitted purposes (personal use without anticipated
  commercial application; non-commercial organisations).
* Links to deleted notes: clicking one showed the server's bare "Not
  Found" page with no way back. It now opens a page in the app: the note's
  old title and deletion date, Restore it (from .trash), the notes still
  linking to it, and an explicit "Turn links into plain text" (history
  kept first). Red links show the deleted note's title. 404 and 500 pages
  are rendered inside the app with Back.
* Citation labels name all authors (bib.py joins authors with "; ", and
  the label split only on " and ", so "Latour and Woolgar 1979" showed as
  "Latour 1979").

## 5.1.0 (November 2026)

* Locked notes. Per-note lock (padlock button, palette) and per-kind lock
  (Settings → Locked notes → Always lock these kinds; existing notes of the
  kind are locked when it's ticked). Body encrypted with AES-256-GCM under a
  one-use key from HKDF-SHA-256 per save; random 256-bit data key wrapped
  under Argon2id(password) (RFC 9106 first recommended setting, falling back
  to the second) and under a 256-bit recovery key. Title, tags, kind,
  threads and dates stay plain.
* Open until quit or an idle time (5/15/30/60 min or never); "Lock now";
  inline unlock in the editor; change password; reset with recovery key;
  remove one lock or all.
* Locked text never enters the search index, links table or embeddings and
  is never sent to AI; index uses SQLite secure_delete and is optimised and
  checkpointed after locking. Export warns that output is unencrypted.
* Wrapped keys in .zetteling-vault.json in the notes folder and a copy in
  every locked note.
* Pages and API replies are sent with Cache-Control: no-store, so decrypted
  text isn't kept in the web view's disk cache.
* POSTs from other websites are refused (Origin/Referer must match), so a
  page in a browser can't drive the local server, e.g. to remove locks.

## 5.0.1 (November 2026)

* Autosave: the editor saves 1.5 s after typing stops and once more when it
  closes (pagehide beacon). Embedding runs only on that last save or on ⌘S.
  New notes are created on their first save, which waits for a title and
  for focus to leave the title field. Per-page draft token and sequence
  number: stale requests are ignored, and racing saves create one note.
  Settings → Writing → Save automatically (default on). "Cancel" reads
  "Done" when autosave is on.
* Pandoc's GPL licence and source pointer now ship inside the app; Licence
  page lists third-party components; THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.
* macOS requires: cryptography<49 (49+ has no Intel macOS wheels).
* Help: create/edit/delete and export instructions updated for the v5
  layout.

## 5.0.0 (November 2026)

* One-window workspace: sidebar, note list, note, side panel (thread,
  backlinks, related notes, Interrogate).
* Settings → Appearance: seven themes (four light, three dark), follow-the-Mac
  mode, accent colour, five bundled fonts, four text sizes, illustrations
  switch. Saved in settings.json.
* Command palette (⌘K) and keyboard shortcuts.
* Editor: [[ note picker and @ citation picker at the cursor; "Links in this
  note" list; unsaved-changes guard.
* Citations render as author and year (the note file keeps @citekey).
* First-run welcome screen for new installs.
* Fixes: export folder and bibliography path chosen in Settings now win over
  environment variables; Gemini defaults updated (old ones were shut down);
  retired-model warnings; HTMX and fonts bundled (no CDN, offline search);
  punctuation-safe search; duplicate renderer removed.

## 4.0.2 (April 2026)

* Replaced bibtexparser with an in-house .bib parser. See the About page.
